// ipspline — the cubic methods, and what each of them is for.
//
// Every body is a `todo()`.  The signatures are the design, and the
// compiler checks them as claims; see the README for the stamp.
//
// FIVE CONSTRUCTORS, AND THE CHOICE BETWEEN THEM IS THE ONLY THING A
// CALLER HAS TO UNDERSTAND ABOUT THIS PACKAGE.  All five answer an
// `IpCurve`; they differ in how the slope at each knot is chosen, and
// that choice is what decides whether the curve wiggles.
//
// THE THREE CLASSICAL SPLINES solve one tridiagonal system for all the
// slopes at once, so that the second derivative is continuous
// everywhere.  That is what "smooth" means and it is also what makes
// them GLOBAL: moving one knot changes the curve everywhere, and a
// single outlier makes the whole curve ring.  They differ only in the
// two extra equations the system needs at the ends:
//
//   natural     second derivative zero at both ends.  The curve is
//               straight as it leaves the data, which is the honest
//               thing for data that has no known slope at its edges.
//   clamped     first derivative given at both ends.  For data whose
//               end slopes are known from physics rather than measured
//               — a trajectory that starts at rest.
//   not-a-knot  third derivative continuous across the SECOND and
//               second-to-last knots, so the first two intervals share
//               one cubic and so do the last two.  scipy's
//               `CubicSpline` default, and the best choice when nothing
//               is known about the ends: it invents no condition and
//               reproduces a true cubic exactly.
//
// THE TWO LOCAL METHODS choose each slope from a few nearby points, so
// there is no system to solve, moving one knot changes the curve only
// nearby, and an outlier stays where it is.  The price is that the
// second derivative is NOT continuous.
//
//   Akima   the slope at a knot is a weighted blend of the two
//           neighbouring secants, weighted so that a knot next to a
//           flat run gets a flat slope.  It is the method for data with
//           a sudden change in it, and it needs five points because the
//           formula looks two knots each way.
//   PCHIP   the slopes are chosen so that the interpolant is MONOTONE
//           on every interval where the data is.  It cannot overshoot:
//           if the data never goes above 1, neither does the curve.
//
// WHICH TO PICK, in one line each: **not-a-knot for smooth data, PCHIP
// when overshoot would be wrong, Akima when the data has a corner in
// it.**  The README has the longer version with the case that makes the
// difference visible.

// The cubic spline with natural end conditions: second derivative zero
// at both ends.
//
// `IpTooFewKnots` below three points — two points have no second
// derivative to set.
//
// ```novo
// use ipknots
// use ipspline
// use ipcurve
//
// fn main() [io]
//     match ipknots.of_lists([0.0, 1.0, 2.0], [0.0, 1.0, 0.0])
//         Ok(k) =>
//             match ipspline.natural(k, IpExtrapPolynomial)
//                 Ok(c)  => println("${ipcurve.method_name(c)}")   // natural
//                 Err(e) => println(e.message())
//         Err(e) => println(e.message())
// ```
pub fn natural(k: IpKnots, e: IpExtrap) -> Result<IpCurve, IpFault> []
    todo("interpolate-nv.ipspline.natural")

// The cubic spline with clamped end conditions: the first derivative is
// `start_slope` at the first knot and `end_slope` at the last.
//
// For data whose end slopes are known rather than measured.
// `IpTooFewKnots` below three points, `IpNotFinite` for a slope that is
// a NaN or an infinity — which would otherwise propagate silently
// through the tridiagonal solve and make the whole curve a NaN.
pub fn clamped(k: IpKnots, start_slope: Float, end_slope: Float,
               e: IpExtrap) -> Result<IpCurve, IpFault> []
    todo("interpolate-nv.ipspline.clamped")

// The cubic spline with not-a-knot end conditions: the third derivative
// is continuous across the second and second-to-last knots.
//
// scipy's `CubicSpline` default, and the right choice when nothing is
// known about the ends: it invents no condition, and it reproduces a
// true cubic exactly, which neither of the other two does.
//
// `IpTooFewKnots` below FOUR points — the condition is about the second
// and second-to-last knots, and with three points those are the same
// knot.
pub fn not_a_knot(k: IpKnots, e: IpExtrap) -> Result<IpCurve, IpFault> []
    todo("interpolate-nv.ipspline.not_a_knot")

// Akima's interpolation: local slopes from a weighted blend of the
// neighbouring secants.
//
// The method for data with a sudden change in it.  A classical spline
// meeting a step rings for several intervals on both sides of it;
// Akima's weighting gives a knot next to a flat run a flat slope, so
// the ringing does not happen.  The price is that the second derivative
// is not continuous.
//
// `IpTooFewKnots` below FIVE points: the formula at a knot looks two
// knots in each direction.  scipy's `Akima1DInterpolator` has the same
// minimum.
pub fn akima(k: IpKnots, e: IpExtrap) -> Result<IpCurve, IpFault> []
    todo("interpolate-nv.ipspline.akima")

// PCHIP: piecewise cubic Hermite with slopes chosen to preserve
// monotonicity.
//
// The method for data where overshoot would be WRONG — a concentration,
// a probability, a count, a cumulative distribution.  A classical
// spline through `[0, 0, 0, 1, 1, 1]` dips below zero before it rises
// and overshoots one after; PCHIP does not, because it picks each slope
// so that the interval's cubic is monotone whenever the data is.
//
// The guarantee is exact and is worth stating precisely: **on any
// interval where the data is monotone, the interpolant is monotone, and
// the interpolant never leaves the range of the data.**  That is the
// property, and it is what the test suite asserts.
//
// `IpTooFewKnots` below three points.
//
// ```novo
// use ipknots
// use ipspline
// use ipcurve
//
// fn main() [io]
//     match ipknots.of_lists([0.0, 1.0, 2.0, 3.0], [0.0, 0.0, 1.0, 1.0])
//         Ok(k) =>
//             match ipspline.pchip(k, IpExtrapClamp)
//                 Ok(c)  => println("${ipcurve.at(c, 0.5) >= 0.0}")   // true
//                 Err(e) => println(e.message())
//         Err(e) => println(e.message())
// ```
pub fn pchip(k: IpKnots, e: IpExtrap) -> Result<IpCurve, IpFault> []
    todo("interpolate-nv.ipspline.pchip")

// The cubic Hermite curve through the knots with slopes the caller
// supplies.
//
// The primitive all five of the above are written on: given a value and
// a slope at each knot, the cubic on each interval is determined.
// Published because a caller who computed slopes some other way — from
// a model, from a measurement, from a previous curve's derivative —
// should not have to reimplement the Hermite basis.
//
// `IpLengthMismatch` when there is not one slope per knot,
// `IpNotFinite` for a slope that is not a number.
pub fn hermite(k: IpKnots, slopes: [Float], e: IpExtrap) -> Result<IpCurve, IpFault> []
    todo("interpolate-nv.ipspline.hermite")

// The slopes PCHIP would choose at each knot, without building a curve.
//
// Published for the caller who wants the monotone slopes and then does
// something else with them, and because it is the one part of PCHIP a
// reader is likely to want to check against Fritsch and Carlson's
// paper directly.
//
// `IpTooFewKnots` below three points.
pub fn pchip_slopes(k: IpKnots) -> Result<[Float], IpFault> []
    todo("interpolate-nv.ipspline.pchip_slopes")

// The slopes Akima's formula would choose at each knot.
//
// The same reasoning as `pchip_slopes`.  `IpTooFewKnots` below five.
pub fn akima_slopes(k: IpKnots) -> Result<[Float], IpFault> []
    todo("interpolate-nv.ipspline.akima_slopes")

// Whether a curve is monotone on the whole of its domain, to within a
// tolerance.
//
// The property `pchip` promises, made checkable.  It is here rather
// than only in the test suite because a caller who built a natural
// spline through data they believed was monotone wants to be able to
// find out that it is not — and because the answer to "did my
// interpolation overshoot" should not require sampling a thousand
// points and looking at a plot.
//
// Checked exactly, from the coefficients: a cubic is monotone on an
// interval when its derivative — a quadratic — does not change sign
// there, which is a discriminant and two roots rather than a sample.
pub fn is_monotone(c: IpCurve, tolerance: Float) -> Bool []
    todo("interpolate-nv.ipspline.is_monotone")

// The curve's smallest and largest values over its whole domain, as a
// pair.
//
// Exact, from the coefficients: a piecewise cubic's extrema are at the
// knots and at the roots of each interval's quadratic derivative, so
// this is arithmetic rather than sampling.
//
// The call that answers "did my interpolation leave the range of my
// data" in one line, which is the question `pchip` exists to make
// unnecessary and the one a natural spline invites.
pub fn range_of(c: IpCurve) -> IpRange []
    todo("interpolate-nv.ipspline.range_of")

// IpRange is a curve's smallest and largest value.
//
// A struct rather than a tuple because `r.0` and `r.1` at a call site
// is how a minimum becomes a maximum.
pub struct IpRange
    // The smallest value the curve takes on its domain.
    lo: Float
    // The largest.
    hi: Float
